{#reference-features}
# Reference features

The Tier-3 set, for handbooks and specifications rather than product docs. All of
it is off by default; this site turns everything on.

## Citations

Carve carries typed locators, so a citation knows that `p. 33` is a page rather
than a string that happens to follow a comma [@knuth1984, p. 33].

A group cites several works at once [@knuth1984; @lamport1994], and an integral
citation reads as part of the sentence: +[@lamport1994] showed the same thing.

[@knuth1984]: Knuth, D. E. (1984). /The TeXbook/. Addison-Wesley.
[@lamport1994]: Lamport, L. (1994). /LaTeX: A Document Preparation System/. Addison-Wesley.

## Glossary

A term marks itself where it is used, and the glossary collects the definitions.

A :term[parser] turns source into a tree, and a :term[renderer] turns that tree
into an output format.

::: glossary
:: parser
:  The pass that reads source text and produces a document tree.

:: renderer
:  The pass that walks a document tree and emits one output format.
:::

## Index

An index entry marks a page position :index[markup language] without showing
anything, and the index collects them.

## Cross-references and numbering

With `heading-numbers` on, sections number themselves and a cross-reference
carries the number: </#reference-features> above, and </#citations>.

## Spoilers

Some documents want an answer hidden until asked for: :spoiler[the parser runs first].

